  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 325 PM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nLooking broadly at the synoptic scale, the remnants of Hurricane \nPolo interacts with an upper-level low over the SW CONUS late \nTuesday into Wednesday, with the entire system moving northeastward \ntowards the Ohio Valley. Over our area, the western edge of the \nridge continues to persist, keeping us dry on Wednesday. With the \nreturn of southerly flow, the end of September will be very warm with \nhighs in the mid to upper 80s and a few places reaching 90.\n\nFor Thursday, a shortwave trough over the Great Lakes region will \ninteract with the surface ridge, with moisture from the previously \nmentioned upper-level low moving northeastward. Conditions will be \ndry for most of the day on Thursday before a cold front associated \nwith the shortwave trough pushes southward towards the region. Rain \nchances will return, lasting through at least late Friday. From the \nlatest LREF, rainfall totals range from 1-1.5\" across the area, with \nhigher amounts off to our west. The remainder of the forecast period \nbecomes uncertain as there is high variability in deterministic and \nensemble guidance. The uncertainty lies in the progression of the \nsurface cold front and associated surface high pressure behind. \nSome guidance has the front pushing well to our south, keeping the \narea mostly dry next weekend, while other model outputs keep the \nfront nearby with persisting rain chances through the weekend. With \nthe high uncertainty, chance (< 50%) POPs are in the forecast \npackage during this time frame.",
